@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex purpose
@value{PROJECT} is a Rust boot splash for Steelbore OS Bravais.  It shows real
boot progress read from systemd, prompts for disk passphrases inside the splash
through the systemd password-agent protocol, and holds the screen until greetd
takes over the virtual terminal.  It is one process started twice: once in the
initrd, where it draws from the first frame simpledrm can give and prompts for
LUKS, and once after switch-root, where it switches to the real
@code{Manager.Progress} figure and holds the last frame until the greeter
claims the VT.  On shutdown it runs a third time.

@cindex fail-open
Fairing is designed to be fail-open.  No boot target requires it, every wait
on an external resource carries a timeout, and a crash leaves the console and
the stock @command{systemd-tty-ask-password-agent} to finish the boot.  The
residual risk is silent wrongness --- a bar that looks right while lying, or a
passphrase mis-forwarded --- which is why the project is assurance Category B
with two subsystems raised to Category A: the password agent
(@code{fairing-askpass}) and the initrd lifecycle unit.

@cindex crates
@cindex workspace
The source is one Cargo workspace of four product crates:

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .55 .2
@headitem Crate @tab Role @tab Category
@item @code{fairing}
@tab Binary: CLI, lifecycle state machine, systemd notify
@tab B
@item @code{fairing-render}
@tab Backend trait with DRM, fbdev and text implementations; frame compositor
@tab B
@item @code{fairing-theme}
@tab Compiled theme format, Nickel contract, @code{theme check} / @code{theme compile}
@tab B
@item @code{fairing-askpass}
@tab systemd ask-password agent: inotify watcher, bounded parser, socket reply, zeroisation
@tab A
@end multitable

@cindex conformance
The project conforms to The Steelbore Standard v2.12 as a Category B project,
tailored; the tailoring register is @file{COMPLIANCE.md} at the repository
root.  This manual carries the two artefacts the Standard places in Texinfo:
the Needs (@pxref{Needs}) and the Requirements (@pxref{Requirements}).  The
Interfaces (ICD, Standard §23) and Dependencies chapters are added at
the G2 design gate.

@node Invoking Fairing
@chapter Invoking Fairing

@cindex command line
@cindex invoking
@command{fairing} follows the Spacecraft Software Dual-Mode Self-Documenting CLI
Standard: every verb answers in human-readable text on a terminal and in a
single JSON document (@code{--json}) when piped, under an agent variable, or
when asked.  The live capability manifest is @command{fairing describe --json};
@command{fairing schema [@var{command}]} returns JSON Schema Draft 2020-12 for
the whole tool or one verb.  This chapter describes the verbs of version
@value{VERSION}.

@menu
* splash::              The boot splash the systemd units run.
* theme::               Check, compile and inspect themes.
* preview::             Draw the splash with a simulated bar.
* describe and schema:: Self-description for people and agents.
* Global flags::        Output mode, colour, severity floor.
* Environment::         Variables that change behaviour.
* Exit codes::          What a non-zero status means.
@end menu

@node splash
@section @command{fairing splash}

@cindex splash
@example
fairing splash --stage initrd|system|shutdown [--theme @var{file.fairing}]
               [--palette @var{slug}] [--no-agent] [--reduced-motion]
@end example

The boot splash itself.  The systemd units run it, once per boot stage
(@pxref{Running under systemd}); a person runs it only to try a theme, and
@command{fairing preview} is the better tool for that.

@table @code
@item --stage initrd
From early boot until switch-root.  The bar follows elapsed time against the
initrd's duration on the previous boot, up to 30 percent; on a first boot it
follows a fixed curve that reaches 30 percent at 8 seconds.  On
@code{SIGTERM} it writes the bar value to @file{/run/fairing/state} for the
next stage and exits.
@item --stage system
From switch-root until greetd starts.  The bar starts from the value the
initrd left and follows systemd's own job-queue progress
(@code{org.freedesktop.systemd1.Manager.Progress}) from 30 to 99 percent; it
reaches 100 percent only at the handoff.  Under the bar it shows the
description of the newest running systemd job.  A @code{SIGTERM} that follows
the handoff marker @file{/run/fairing/handoff}, which greetd's start writes,
is the handoff: the splash shows the full bar, releases the display within
100 ms, writes the measured stage durations to
@file{/var/lib/fairing/boot-duration} and exits.  Any other @code{SIGTERM}
releases the display just as fast, without the full bar or the
measurement.
@item --stage shutdown
From @code{shutdown.target} until the final kill: the theme's shutdown layout,
its logo and one line (``Shutting down'' in the built-in theme), with no bar.
Nothing but a signal ends it, and it asks no password question.
@item --theme
A compiled theme (@pxref{Themes}).  Without it the built-in theme is drawn.
@item --palette
A registered palette slug, as for @command{fairing preview}.
@item --no-agent
Do not answer password questions; leave them to systemd's console agent.
Without it the splash answers them itself (@pxref{Password agent}).
@item --reduced-motion
Keep the theme's frame sequence on its first frame and move the bar without
easing (@pxref{Accessibility}).
@end table

Escape shows the journal over the splash (@pxref{Journal overlay}).

@cindex fail-open
The bar never moves backwards: when a new reading is lower it holds, and a
higher one is approached smoothly.  A splash never fails the boot.  Every way
out exits with status 0 and the reason in the report: @code{handoff},
@code{stopped} (stopped in stage 2 without the greetd handoff, for example by
a password prompt under @option{--no-agent}), @code{boot-finished} (the boot ended without a greeter,
for example when booted to @code{multi-user.target}), @code{switch-root}, @code{shutdown}, @code{failed-unit} (a
systemd unit failed), @code{maintenance} (rescue or emergency mode started),
@code{no-backend}, @code{render-failed}, @code{agent-failed} (the password
agent could not start, or lost its question directory, so systemd's console
agent asks instead) or @code{theme-unreadable}.  When it
draws nothing it still tells systemd it is ready, so the unit never delays
the boot.  A page flip that completes late costs the frames drawn while it is
pending, not the splash: only a display that has not completed a flip for two
seconds ends it with @code{render-failed}.

@cindex journal
Under systemd, diagnostics go to the journal as one line each, with a syslog
priority: a missing display and a missing D-Bus connection are
@code{notice}, other trouble is @code{warning}.  The report goes to the
journal too, as one JSON document.  Besides what was drawn it carries the
waits the splash timed, each the longest of the run or @code{null}:
@code{release_ms} (SIGTERM to the output released), @code{reacquire_ms} (a lost
output, such as the simpledrm-to-native swap, to its next frame),
@code{ask_detect_ms} (a question's file written to the agent opening it),
@code{grab_release_ms} (the agent frame that answers a question and releases
the keyboards) and @code{overlay_entry_ms} (a journal entry to the shown
overlay).  These are the reference-machine budgets (@pxref{Budgets}).

@node theme
@section @command{fairing theme}

@cindex theme check
@cindex theme compile
@cindex theme inspect
@example
fairing theme check @var{theme.ncl}
fairing theme compile @var{theme.ncl} [--output @var{file.fairing}]
fairing theme inspect @var{file.fairing}
@end example

@table @code
@item check
Evaluates the theme against the contract and applies every rule of
@code{compile}, writing nothing.  A violation exits 2 and carries Nickel's own
diagnostic, as plain text, in the error's @code{detail}.
@item compile
Writes the artefact the splash loads, by default beside the source with
@file{.fairing} in place of @file{.ncl}.  The report gives the size and the
median time this machine takes to load the artefact.  @code{--dry-run}
compiles and reports without writing.
@item inspect
Reads an artefact and shows its layouts and images.
@end table

@code{check} and @code{compile} need the Nickel evaluator, which only the
full build carries.  The builds the NixOS module runs at boot do not, and
answer @code{FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE}; they load compiled themes only.

@node preview
@section @command{fairing preview}

@cindex preview
@cindex simulated bar
@example
fairing preview [--seconds @var{n}] [--backend auto|drm|fbdev|memory]
                [--snapshot @var{file.ppm}] [--palette @var{slug}]
                [--status @var{text}] [--size @var{W}x@var{H}] [--fps @var{hz}]
                [--theme @var{file}] [--reduced-motion]
@end example

Draws the splash with a bar that advances from 0 to 100 percent over
@var{n} seconds (default 5; fractions are accepted and @code{0} draws a single
frame), then restores the console.  Theme authors use it to iterate without
rebooting.  The same compositor and backends serve the boot splash.

@table @code
@item --backend
@code{auto} tries DRM/KMS first and falls back to @file{/dev/fb0}; the other
values try one backend only.  @code{memory} renders off-screen and is the only
backend an agent environment may use (@pxref{Environment}).
@item --snapshot
Writes the final frame as a binary PPM (@code{P6}, 8 bits per channel) to the
given path.  The frame is written to a sibling @file{.part} file and renamed
into place once complete, so a failed run leaves an existing file untouched.
The format needs no library and every image viewer opens it.
@item --palette
A registered Steelbore theme slug such as @code{steelbore},
@code{steelbore-high-contrast} or @code{tokyonight}: the explicit source of
the Steelbore Standard §11.6 resolution, reported as @code{command-line}.  It
outranks @env{SPACECRAFT_THEME}; an unregistered slug is a usage error.
Without it the palette is resolved as §11.6 prescribes: the environment
variable, then the theme's declared palette (with @code{--theme}), then the
family default, with @env{NO_COLOR} (or @code{--no-color})
overlaying the mono theme.  A @code{-high-contrast} or mono slug named here or
in @env{SPACECRAFT_THEME} is pinned and no overlay changes it.  The theme is
resolved once and held for the run.
@item --status
The status line drawn under the bar; the boot splash shows the description of
the systemd job in progress here.
@item --size
The frame size for the memory backend (default @code{1920x1080}), each side
@code{1} to @code{16384} and the whole frame at most 256 MiB.  A device
backend always draws at the connector's preferred mode.
@item --fps
The redraw rate, 1 to 240 frames per second (default 30).
@item --theme
A compiled theme, or a Nickel source that is compiled in memory first (full
build only).  Without it the built-in theme is drawn.
@item --dry-run
Reports which backends would be tried, in order, with which palette, without
touching a device.  A device's mode is not guessed: @code{width},
@code{height} and @code{format} are null in the plan.  The envelope carries
@code{"dry_run": true}.
@end table

@cindex layout
The layout is authored in a 1920x1080 reference frame and scaled
uniformly into the active mode, centred, with the uncovered bands painted in
the canvas colour.  The percentage is always rendered as text beside the bar,
so the state is never carried by colour alone.

@cindex report
In machine mode the @code{data} object reports @code{backend} (@code{auto}
only in a plan, where the chain decides at run time), the @code{chain} tried
in order, @code{device}, @code{width}, @code{height}, @code{format}, the
resolved @code{palette}
(@code{slug}, @code{base}, @code{source}, @code{overlay}, @code{skipped}),
@code{theme} (its name, @code{builtin} without @code{--theme}),
@code{seconds}, @code{fps}, @code{frames}, @code{dropped},
@code{first_frame_ms}, @code{measured_fps}, @code{snapshot}, the
@code{fallbacks} that were tried before the working backend, and
@code{planned}.

@cindex console, restoring
On the DRM path the CRTC is returned to the framebuffer it scanned out before;
on the fbdev path the device contents are saved on open and written back on
close, and the console cursor blink is paused meanwhile.  Both restorations run
on every exit path, including a failure midway through the run.  Both paths
need the process to be the DRM master or to own the framebuffer: run on a text
console, not under a desktop session.  A snapshot path is opened before any
device, so a bad path fails without switching the console.

@node describe and schema
@section @command{fairing describe} and @command{fairing schema}

@cindex describe
@cindex schema
@command{fairing describe} emits the capability manifest: every verb with its
idempotency, destructiveness and dry-run support, the global flags, the output
formats, and the detected reader profile.  It has no side effects.
@command{fairing schema} with no argument emits the schema of the whole tool;
with a verb name it emits that verb's parameters, output schema, exit codes and
examples, all derived from the same argument parser that runs the verb, so the
two cannot drift.

@node Global flags
@section Global flags

@cindex flags, global
@table @code
@item --json
@itemx --format human|json|jsonl|yaml|csv|explore
Output mode.  @code{yaml}, @code{csv} and @code{explore} are accepted and
answered with @code{FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE}, or with a @code{TUI_FALLBACK} warning
plus JSON for @code{explore}.
@item --fields @var{f1,f2,...}
Keep only the named top-level keys of @code{data}.
@item --dry-run
Report the plan without side effects, where the verb supports it.
@item --verbose
@itemx --quiet
Lower the diagnostic floor to @code{info}, or raise it to @code{error}.
Mutually exclusive.
@item --no-color
@itemx --color auto|always|never
Colour policy for human mode.
@item --absolute-time
Timestamps as ISO 8601 UTC in human mode (the only form Fairing uses anyway).
@item --print0
NUL-delimited output for @command{xargs -0}.
@item --yes
@itemx --force
Skip confirmations.
@item --version
Name, version, maintainer, website and copyright, as text or as an envelope.
@item --accessible
@itemx --no-accessible
Turn accessible mode on, or keep it off whatever the environment and the
kernel command line say (@pxref{Accessibility}).
@end table

@node Environment
@section Environment

@cindex environment variables
@cindex agents
@table @env
@item AI_AGENT
@itemx AGENT
Set to any non-empty value other than @code{0} or @code{false}: machine
output, no colour, no prompts, no virtual terminal, diagnostic floor
@code{warn}.
@item CI
Truthy: machine output, no colour, no virtual terminal.
@item CLAUDECODE
@itemx CURSOR_AGENT
@itemx GEMINI_CLI
Informational: recorded as @code{metadata.tool_agent}; colour, prompts and
the virtual terminal are off, the output format is unchanged.
@item NO_COLOR
@itemx FORCE_COLOR
@itemx CLICOLOR
@itemx TERM
Colour precedence as the CLI Standard defines it.  @env{NO_COLOR} also selects
the mono theme for the splash itself.
@item SPACECRAFT_THEME
A registered theme slug for the splash palette (Steelbore Standard §11.6).
@item SPACECRAFT_A11Y
@code{1} turns accessible mode on, @code{0} keeps it off; below
@option{--accessible} and above the kernel command line.
@item NOTIFY_SOCKET
Set by systemd for a @code{Type=notify} unit: @command{fairing splash} sends
@code{READY=1} there after its first frame and @code{STOPPING=1} as it exits.
@item JOURNAL_STREAM
Set by systemd when standard error is the journal: diagnostics are then
written as journal lines with a syslog priority.
@end table

@cindex kernel command line
The splash also reads three kernel parameters: @code{fairing.theme=@var{slug}},
which chooses the palette below @code{--palette} and above
@env{SPACECRAFT_THEME}; @code{fairing.accessible=1} or @code{=0}, accessible
mode below @env{SPACECRAFT_A11Y}; and @code{fairing.reduced_motion=1}.

@node Exit codes
@section Exit codes

@cindex exit codes
@multitable @columnfractions .1 .3 .6
@headitem Code @tab Error codes @tab Meaning
@item 0 @tab @tab Success.
@item 1 @tab @code{FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE}, @code{INTERNAL_ERROR}
@tab Not built into this release, or an unexpected failure.
@item 2 @tab @code{INVALID_ARGUMENT}, @code{MISSING_ARGUMENT}
@tab Usage error, including a device backend requested under an agent variable.
@item 3 @tab @code{NOT_FOUND}
@tab No DRM device or @file{/dev/fb0} to draw on, a snapshot path in a
directory that does not exist, or a @command{schema} target that is not a verb.
@item 4 @tab @code{PERMISSION_DENIED}
@tab The device refused the process: not in the @code{video} group, or no
@code{CAP_SYS_ADMIN} to become DRM master.
@item 5 @tab @code{CONFLICT}
@tab Another client holds DRM master, such as a running compositor; stop it or
run on a text console.
@end multitable

Every non-zero exit in machine mode writes one line
@code{@{"error":@{code,exit_code,message,hint,timestamp,command@}@}} to
standard error; @code{hint} is a runnable command.  An error may also carry
@code{detail}, text another tool produced verbatim, such as Nickel's
diagnostic for a theme that breaks the contract.

@command{fairing splash} is the exception: it exits 0 in every case it can
reach, so a splash can never be the reason a boot fails.  Its arguments are
still checked, and a usage error is still exit 2.

@node Themes
@chapter Themes

@cindex theme
@cindex Nickel
@cindex contract
A theme is a Nickel file that satisfies the contract
@file{contracts/fairing-theme.ncl}.  Fairing applies the contract itself, so
the theme need not import it; importing it as well lets an editor check the
file as it is written.  The reference theme, @file{themes/steelbore.ncl}, is
the built-in layout written out in full and is the place to start.

@cindex reference frame
All geometry is in the 1920x1080 reference frame and is scaled uniformly to
the active mode at boot.  A theme declares:

@table @code
@item name
Shown in reports.
@item palette
The registered Steelbore palette the theme is drawn in, such as
@code{steelbore} or @code{tokyonight}.  It is the theme's default: the palette
can still be chosen at boot (@pxref{Palette selection}).
@item layouts.boot
The logo box, the bar (box, corner radius, outline, and the colours of fill,
track and border), the percentage (gap and size), the status line (whether it
is shown, its top and size), the prompt region, and optionally a frame
sequence.
@item layouts.shutdown
The logo, a status line and its message, and optionally a frame sequence.
@end table

@cindex colour
@cindex role
Colours are never written as values.  A colour is a Steelbore role such as
@code{'accent} or @code{'foreground}, or the name of a token of the theme's
own palette, @code{@{ token = "Plasma Orange" @}}.  @command{fairing theme
compile} refuses a literal colour anywhere in the theme, a token of any other
palette, text drawn in a surface colour, and a bar whose fill does not reach
3:1 contrast with its track.  Because the compiled theme stores roles, not
colours, the same artefact draws the high-contrast and mono variants of its
palette without any extra file.

@cindex logo
@cindex frame sequence
A logo is @code{'builtin}, Fairing's vector mark, or a PNG file named
relative to the theme.  Every opaque pixel of a PNG must be a colour of the
declared palette, unless the logo sets @code{tint}, in which case only the
image's alpha channel is used and drawn in that colour.  A frame sequence is
a list of PNG frames of one size, played at up to 30 frames per second, under
the same rule.  The splash plays it in a loop at its declared rate, from the
first frame; under reduced motion it shows the first frame only.

@cindex artefact
The compiled artefact is one file of at most 4 MiB: an eight-byte magic,
a format version, the layouts in postcard encoding, and the images as palette
role indices or alpha masks.  It loads in well under 5 ms.  A version the
running splash does not know is refused, and the splash then steps aside
rather than draw a wrong theme.

@node Palette selection
@section Palette selection

@cindex palette, selection
@cindex Steelbore Standard §11.6
The splash chooses its palette in two stages.  The base palette is the first
registered slug among: @code{--palette} (the NixOS module's @code{palette}
option), the kernel parameter @code{fairing.theme=}, @env{SPACECRAFT_THEME},
the theme's declared @code{palette}, and the family default
@code{steelbore}.  An unregistered slug is skipped with a warning, never
fatal; the module also refuses an unregistered @code{palette} option at build
time.  @command{fairing preview}, run by a person, treats an unregistered
@code{--palette} as a usage error instead.  Then,
unless a @code{-high-contrast} or mono slug was named outright, @env{NO_COLOR}
selects the mono variant, and otherwise accessible mode selects the
palette's @code{-high-contrast} sibling.

@node Running under systemd
@chapter Running under systemd

@cindex systemd units
@cindex NixOS module
On Steelbore OS Bravais the splash is enabled with one option of the NixOS
module @code{steelbore.fairing}, exported by the repository's flake as
@code{nixosModules.fairing}:

@example
steelbore.fairing = @{
  enable = true;
  kmsModules = [ "i915" ];          # the machine's KMS driver
  # theme = ./my-theme/theme.ncl;   # default: the reference theme
  # palette = "steelbore-high-contrast";
  # agent.enable = false;           # leave passwords to the console agent
@};
@end example

Enabling it compiles the theme at build time, copies the artefact and a small
build of Fairing into the systemd initrd, adds @code{simpledrm} and the
listed KMS drivers to the initrd (and @code{evdev}, through which the password
agent and the journal overlay read keyboards, with @command{journalctl} for
the overlay), adds @code{quiet} and @code{splash} to the
kernel command line, and installs four units.  The module requires the
systemd initrd and greetd, and refuses a configuration that also enables
Plymouth or names an unregistered palette.

@cindex fairing-initrd.service
@table @code
@item fairing-initrd.service
Runs @code{fairing splash --stage initrd} in the initrd, after the kernel
modules are loaded and udev has been triggered.  It has no default
dependencies and is only wanted by @code{initrd.target}.  It is stopped
before switch-root, and leaves @file{/run/fairing} behind for the next stage.
@item fairing.service
Runs @code{fairing splash --stage system} after switch-root, as soon as the
local file systems are mounted (its sandbox needs them), and only when
@file{/run/fairing} exists: a restart of
the boot targets on a running system, as @command{nixos-rebuild switch} does,
never brings the splash back over a session.  It runs with least privilege:
only the capabilities to hold the display and the console, the file system
read-only except its own two directories, and no network.
@item fairing-handoff.service
Runs once logins are allowed, just before greetd: writes the handoff marker,
stops @code{fairing.service} and waits for it to exit.
@item fairing-shutdown.service
@cindex shutdown splash
Runs @code{fairing splash --stage shutdown} whenever @code{shutdown.target}
is reached for a halt, a power-off, a reboot or a kexec.  It starts once the
user sessions, greetd and any boot splash have let the screen go, and the
service that halts, powers off or reboots the machine waits until its first
frame is drawn.  Nothing stops it: it neither conflicts with
@code{shutdown.target} nor has default dependencies, so it draws until
@command{systemd-shutdown} kills every process at the very end.  It runs in
the same sandbox as @code{fairing.service}, without its directories.
@end table

@cindex greetd
@cindex handoff
The handoff to greetd is ordering plus one stop.  @code{fairing.service} is
ordered before @code{greetd.service}, and greetd's start pulls in
@code{fairing-handoff.service}, which writes the handoff marker
@file{/run/fairing/handoff}, then stops @code{fairing.service} and waits for
it, so the splash has drawn the full bar and released the display before
greetd opens the console.  The handoff unit is ordered after
@code{systemd-user-sessions.service}, as greetd is (and as Plymouth's quit
unit is), so the splash holds the screen until the greeter is next; ordered
after the splash alone, it would run as soon as the splash was ready and end
it at the start of stage 2.  The stop is a unit of its own because greetd is
@code{Type=idle}: systemd would hold any command of greetd's for its idle
wait of five seconds.  The two units do
not conflict: in one boot transaction a conflict makes systemd drop greetd's
start job, and the machine would come up without a login screen.

@cindex fail-open
Neither unit is required by any target; a splash that fails, hangs or is
absent cannot stop a boot.  A password question (for example an encrypted
volume) is answered in the splash (@pxref{Password agent}); with
@code{agent.enable = false} it stops the splash instead, so the console
agent's prompt is visible.  The splash's own waits are all bounded: five
seconds to start, one second to stop.

@cindex D-Bus
The initrd build of Fairing carries no D-Bus client, so the initrd splash
learns of trouble only through its conflict with emergency mode.  A failed
unit stops the stage-2 splash, counted from its first reading of systemd, so
units that failed earlier do not.  The splash hears of a failure the moment
it happens, from systemd's own signals: a queued rescue or emergency target,
or a count of failed units that stays above the first reading for 200 ms (a
service restarted after a failed start is counted failed only for an
instant); a poll four times a second backs them up.  After switch-root the splash connects to the system bus; if no
connection is made within 2 seconds, it says so once in the journal and
drives the bar from the previous boot's measured duration instead.

@node Password agent
@chapter Password agent

@cindex password agent
@cindex LUKS
Fairing answers systemd's password questions, such as the passphrase of a
LUKS root, inside the splash: the question is drawn in the theme's prompt
region and typed on the keyboard, and the console never shows.  The agent is
the crate @code{fairing-askpass}, assurance Category A; it runs in the
splash's own loop, one more input read once per frame.

@menu
* Answering a question:: What the agent does, step by step.
* Safety argument::      Why a passphrase does not leak.
@end menu

@node Answering a question
@section Answering a question

@cindex ask-password
A program that needs a password (@command{systemd-cryptsetup}, for one)
writes a file @file{ask.@var{random}} to @file{/run/systemd/ask-password}
and waits on a datagram socket named in it.  Each frame, the agent:

@enumerate
@item
drains the inotify events of that directory without blocking, and when any
@file{ask.*} name came, went or changed (and four times a second in any
case) takes the lowest-named valid question that has not expired and whose
asking process is still alive.  A question already open stays open while its
file is there, so a new one never takes over a prompt mid-typing.  A new
question is seen within one frame of being written.
@item
when a question opens, grabs every keyboard exclusively, so no keystroke
reaches the console, and discards anything typed before the prompt; a
keyboard plugged in later is grabbed within a second.  A keyboard is any
input device that reports every key from Esc to D, as udev decides it.
@item
types into a fixed buffer of 512 bytes through a US layout: Backspace
deletes, Enter answers, Escape cancels.  Ctrl and Alt chords type nothing.
Because the layout is fixed, the NixOS module turns the agent off by default
when the console keymap is not US, and warns when it is forced on there.
@item
on Enter, sends @samp{+} and the passphrase as one datagram to the
question's socket, on Escape a single @samp{-}, then clears the buffer and
releases the keyboards in the same frame.
@end enumerate

The prompt shows the question's message and one dot per character typed, or
the text itself for a question asked with @code{Echo=1}; a question asked
with @code{Silent=1} shows neither, not even the count.  When a question
with the same @code{Id=} comes back within 30 seconds of an answer (the key
derivation rejected the passphrase), the prompt says it was not accepted.  A
question whose @code{NotAfter=} passes, or whose file is removed, closes
with nothing sent.

@cindex fail-open
While the agent is up, @file{/run/fairing/agent} exists, and the NixOS module
conditions systemd's console agent on its absence, both its directory watch
(the @code{.path} unit) and its service, as systemd itself does for Plymouth.
Once the splash is ready, its unit starts the watch again so the condition is
checked with the agent up; a watch left running would fire the skipped service
at every question until it hit its trigger limit and failed.  Every way out of
the splash, a crash included, removes the file and starts the watch again, so
a question still open is asked on the console.  For the same reason the
splash ends with reason @code{agent-failed} when its agent cannot start, when
a question has been open for 10 seconds with no keyboard to answer it, and
when a question is open but the output has been lost for 2 seconds, rather
than take an answer nobody can see.

@node Safety argument
@section Safety argument

@cindex safety argument
This is the analysis evidence for the agent's secrecy and resource
requirements (FRN-SRS-024, FRN-SRS-025, FRN-SRS-102, FRN-SRS-104).  Each
claim names the mechanism and the test or check that holds it.

@table @asis
@item One place for the secret
The typed passphrase lives in one type, @code{Secret}: 512 bytes inline,
never on the heap, with no @code{Clone}, @code{Copy} or @code{Display}; its
@code{Debug} shows only the count.  Nothing outside the crate sees its bytes:
the splash draws from a view that carries the count typed, or the text only
for an @code{Echo=1} question.  An echoed answer may still be a passphrase
(crypttab @code{password-echo=yes}), so the splash carries it in a fixed
buffer of its own, zeroised when cleared and when dropped, and the
compositor draws from a slice of it, never a copy.  The reply builds its
datagram in a fixed buffer on the stack.

@item Cleared eagerly, on every path
Release builds abort on panic, which skips @code{Drop}, so the agent clears
the buffer explicitly wherever a question ends: before the answer is sent,
on cancel, on expiry, when the question is withdrawn or replaced, and when
the agent closes.  The datagram buffer is zeroised as soon as it is sent or
fails to send.  Clearing uses volatile writes (@code{zeroize}) the optimiser
cannot remove; @code{Drop} clears again as a second line.  Unit tests check
every path.

@item Keystrokes
Key events are read with a plain @code{read(2)} into a stack buffer that is
zeroised after every read; the @code{evdev} crate only issues the grab.
While a prompt is open the grab keeps keystrokes from the console's line
discipline, so none can be read back from a tty.

@item Not in the journal, not in a core
The agent's journal lines say that a question opened, was answered,
cancelled or closed, and nothing about what was typed, not even its length;
the LUKS VM test searches the whole boot's journal for the passphrase.  The
agent marks the process non-dumpable when it opens, which also keeps an
unprivileged @code{ptrace} out, and both units set @code{LimitCORE=0}.

@item Bounded input, bounded memory
An @file{ask.*} file larger than 64 KiB, or not UTF-8, is refused before any
field is read.  Every field is copied into a fixed buffer: the message is cut
at a character boundary, and an over-long @code{Id=} or socket path refuses
the question.  Files are read into one buffer of 64 KiB and a byte, allocated
when the agent starts.  The crate's @file{clippy.toml} refuses
@code{String}, @code{format!}, whole-file reads and growth by content, and
CI denies warnings, so no allocation sized by what a question contains can
come back unnoticed.  The parser runs under libFuzzer in CI.

@item Only systemd's own socket
systemd writes @code{Message=} raw, after @code{Socket=} and @code{Echo=}, so
a newline in a device label starts a new line of the file.  Read the usual
way, with the last value winning, a label holding a second @code{Socket=}
would send the passphrase elsewhere.  A key the parser reads that appears
twice refuses the whole question instead.  The agent also answers only a
socket directly inside the question directory, where systemd always creates
it, and sends nothing when that path is a link rather than a socket.  A
refused question is not handed to the console agent, which reads the file
the usual way; it stays unanswered, which fails closed.

@item What the splash keeps of an echoed answer
An @code{Echo=1} answer reaches the compositor in a fixed buffer zeroised on
clear and drop.  The glyphs drawn for it stay in the renderer's cache only
while its prompt is open: on the first frame after, every cached glyph is
zeroised and the cache emptied, so the set of characters the answer used is
not kept for the rest of the boot.  Caps Lock is read from the keyboard's
LED when the grab starts, so a passphrase typed with Caps Lock already on is
sent as typed.
@end table

@cindex residual risk
What the argument does not cover: copies the compiler makes in registers and
spilled stack slots, which no library can zeroise; the kernel's own buffers
(the input queue, the socket); the program that receives the answer; and,
after switch-root, swap, since locking the buffer in memory needs
@code{unsafe}, which Fairing forbids.  The initrd has no swap.

@node Journal overlay
@chapter Journal overlay

@cindex journal overlay
@cindex Escape
Escape shows the journal over the splash, in the initrd, in stage 2 and at
shutdown alike: the boot's last 40 entries at priority @code{notice} or
higher, newest at the bottom, each one line of plain text with the seconds
since boot, a severity tag (@code{[ERROR]}, @code{[WARN]} or @code{[INFO]})
and the program that wrote it.  New entries appear as they are written.
Escape again hides it.

@table @kbd
@item Up
@itemx k
One entry back.
@item Down
@itemx j
One entry forward.
@item PgUp
@itemx PgDn
Ten entries back or forward.
@end table

@cindex keyboard, no grab
The overlay reads the keyboards without taking them: every key also reaches
the console as it would with no splash, so @kbd{Ctrl+Alt+F@var{n}}, a screen
reader's keys and SysRq keep working, and keys held with Ctrl, Alt or Meta
are left alone.  While a password question is open the overlay is hidden and
Escape belongs to the question.  The splash runs @command{journalctl} for the
overlay the first time it is shown and stops it when the splash ends.  If it
cannot run, the overlay says so instead of showing entries.

@node Accessibility
@chapter Accessibility

@cindex accessible mode
@cindex text backend
@cindex screen reader
Accessible mode replaces the graphics with plain text on the console, which
a screen reader, a braille display through @command{brltty} or a serial
console can read.  It is off unless one of three triggers turns it on, in
this order of precedence: the @option{--accessible} flag (or
@option{--no-accessible} to keep it off), the environment variable
@env{SPACECRAFT_A11Y} (@code{1} or @code{0}), and the kernel parameter
@code{fairing.accessible} (@code{1} or @code{0}).  An explicit off wins
over every source below it.  Under @option{--verbose} the splash reports the
mode and the source that decided it.

In accessible mode the splash writes one line, @samp{Fairing @var{NN}%
@var{status}}, when the progress or the status changes, at most once a
second, to @file{/dev/console}; at shutdown the line is @samp{Fairing
Shutting down}.  A password question is written as the plain text of its
message, and a rejected answer as one more line; what is typed is never
written, not even as a count.  Every control character is replaced by a
space, so no colour or cursor sequence reaches the console.  The splash opens
the console without making it its controlling terminal and never waits on
it.  @command{fairing preview --accessible} writes the same lines to
standard error.  Accessible mode also selects the palette's high-contrast
sibling.

A question is answered from a keyboard attached to the machine, as in
graphical mode: the splash does not read a serial line.  On a machine
answered only over a serial console, turn the splash's agent off
(@code{agent.enable = false}) so systemd's console agent asks there.  If the
console takes no text for two seconds while a question is open (a terminal
stopped with Scroll Lock, say), the splash steps aside and leaves the
question to systemd's console agent.  The lines are UTF-8, as the Linux
console expects; a serial terminal set to an 8-bit character set may read
some letters as control codes.

@cindex reduced motion
Reduced motion keeps a theme's frame sequence on its first frame and moves
the bar straight to each new value instead of easing toward it.  Accessible
mode always has it; on its own it is @option{--reduced-motion} or the kernel
parameter @code{fairing.reduced_motion=1}.

@node Budgets
@chapter Budgets

@cindex budgets
@cindex margins
@cindex reference machine
Every resource Fairing must keep within a bound has a ceiling, stated by a
requirement and declared once in @file{doc/budgets.toml}; the figures are in
@file{doc/measurements.toml} (Steelbore Standard §22).  The margin is the
headroom left, @math{(ceiling - measured) / ceiling}: green above 25 percent,
amber from 10 to 25, red below 10, exceeded below zero.  A red or exceeded
margin blocks the release.  The two sizes are measured in CI on every build,
and @command{cargo xtask budgets} gates them there; the latencies are measured
on the reference machine, where @command{cargo xtask measure} takes them from
the splash reports of a few boots and from @command{fairing preview} and
@command{fairing theme compile}, as @file{RELEASING.md} describes.  The table below is generated by
@command{cargo xtask budgets-texi} from the committed figures.

