@node Releasing
@chapter Releasing

@cindex release archive
@cindex packaging
@cindex SBOM
@cindex release manifest
A release of Fairing is a source archive, its bill of materials and a
manifest, each with a SHA-256 checksum, cut from a signed tag (Steelbore
Standard §5.5, §24.3, §25.3).  The maintainer's procedure is in
@file{RELEASING.md}; this chapter says what each piece is and how to build
Fairing from it.

@section The source archive

@command{cargo xtask dist} writes @file{target/dist/fairing-@var{version}.tar.gz}:
the tree at a commit, from @command{git archive}, with every crate it builds
from vendored under @file{vendor/} by @command{cargo vendor --locked} and
@file{.cargo/config.toml} pointing cargo at them.  It builds with no network,
as Nix and Guix require of a build.  It is bit-reproducible: entries sorted,
owners and modes normalised, every timestamp the commit time of the last
commit to change what the archive holds, and gzip without a name or a time.
@command{cargo vendor} writes differently from one cargo release to the
next, so @command{cargo xtask dist} runs only with the cargo
@file{rust-toolchain.toml} pins, and only in a full clone.

The three package definitions carry the archive's version and checksum, each
in its own notation: an SRI hash in @file{packaging/default.nix}, Nix base-32
in @file{packaging/guix.scm} and hexadecimal in @file{packaging/PKGBUILD}.
@command{cargo xtask dist} writes them; @command{cargo xtask dist --check}
fails unless they already carry the checksum of the archive it rebuilds.
@file{packaging/}, the release manifests and the flake are
@code{export-ignore} in @file{.gitattributes}, so recording a checksum
changes nothing the archive holds; the archive carries the sources and their
crates, and the package definitions are used from a checkout of the tag.

@section Installing a release

In a checkout of the release tag:

@table @asis
@item Nix
@file{packaging/default.nix} without arguments fetches the release archive
and builds the crates vendored in it:
@example
nix-build -E 'with import <nixpkgs> @{@}; callPackage ./packaging/default.nix @{@}'
@end example
The flake's packages and its @code{nixosModules.fairing} build the flake's own
tree instead, from @file{Cargo.lock}.
@item Guix
@example
guix build -f packaging/guix.scm
guix package --install-from-file=packaging/guix.scm
@end example
@item Arch Linux
With @file{PKGBUILD} in an empty directory, as a user who may run
@command{sudo pacman}:
@example
makepkg --syncdeps --install
@end example
@end table

Each installs the @command{fairing} binary and this manual in Info form.  The
boot integration (the units, the initrd and the greetd handoff) is the NixOS
module's, described in @ref{Running under systemd}; the Guix and Arch packages
give the command line only.

@section Bill of materials

@command{cargo xtask sbom} writes @file{fairing-@var{version}.cdx.json}, a
CycloneDX 1.5 description of the @command{fairing} binary and every crate it
links, for every target, from the committed lockfile.  cargo-cyclonedx's
timestamp, random serial number and checkout path are replaced by the
archive's content time, a UUID derived from the document and @samp{.}, so the
same tree gives the same bytes.  An advisory names the affected releases by
their SBOMs.

@section The manifest

@command{cargo xtask manifest} writes
@file{doc/releases/fairing-@var{version}.manifest.json}, committed at the
release tag and published with it: the identity and assurance category, every
artefact with its checksum (refused unless the package definitions carry the
same one), the toolchain and nixpkgs pins and the release profile, the
requirement counts with coverage and mutation figures, the budgets with their
margins, the known issues and the conformance claim.  The last three, and the
validation note, come from @file{doc/releases/@var{version}.toml}, which the
maintainer writes.  The release workflow rebuilds the archive and the SBOM
from the tag and runs @command{cargo xtask manifest --check} before it drafts
the release.

@section Evidence gates

@table @command
@item cargo xtask coverage
Reads the JSON summary of @command{cargo llvm-cov} and fails a product crate
whose line coverage is under 80 percent.  The files that only run against a
device are left out by name, each with its reason; there is one,
@file{crates/fairing-render/src/backend/drm.rs}.
@item cargo xtask mutants --outcomes @var{file}
Reads cargo-mutants' @file{outcomes.json} and fails on a surviving mutant that
@file{doc/mutants.toml} does not triage as equivalent or unobservable, with
the reason.  The @code{mutants} workflow runs the password agent and the
splash lifecycle weekly and on demand.
@end table

